KG42.register("nodes-b578b1d160f6d0d6d6357717",{"id":"COMP-a607cc256fbfc1eb","code":"SET038","label":"Electricity revenue-cost accounting at price times quantity","definition":"Calculate payable charges or receivable income according to the price and electricity-transaction billing quantity specified by the source, retaining separately the sign, measurement basis, quantity limit, and accumulation scope.","layer":"L8","template_code":"JOC","concept_id":"settlement.payment","concept_ids":["settlement.payment"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Economic purpose: calculate purchase and sale electricity transaction payments","Main object: individual electricity bill, sales revenue, or local transaction income and expenditure","Responsibility relationship: determine the meaning of receipt and payment according to the participant's purchase or sale role"]},"exclusions":[["BP_{2P}^i=∫_{t=1}^{T}(NP_t^i−P_{aggBC,t}^i+P_{aggBD,t}^i)Pr^t dt; retain the negative sign before the charging term, the positive sign before the discharging term, and the integration range from 1 to T.","If the net position in the parentheses is negative, use Pr_{sell}^t; if positive, use Pr_{buy}^t; the price choice for a zero net position is not listed.","A positive bill value is payable, and a negative value is income; settlement is based on smart-meter import and export records after 24 h of consumption, and half-hour records may be used.","ESC manages bills and ensures that prosumer income and consumer expenditure are balanced.","P2G is only a comparison bill: BP_{2G}^i=∫_{t=1}^{T}(λ_{buy,t}P_{im,t}^i−λ_{sell,t}P_{ex,t}^i)dt.","The source reports that without compensation some prosumers are harmed; appropriate compensation can benefit all customers, but savings or income may be uneven.","The result BP_{2P}^i\u003cBP_{2G}^i for consumers without exports under the purchase-price cap condition is retained as the source's comparison conclusion."],["Case 1：payment^{DNEM}_{LA,Case1}=λ_0^{Case1}*P_l^{imp,Case1}=140 $/MWh×15 MW=2100 $。","The income corresponds to LA selling the awarded import quantity in DNEM; it is sales revenue and does not deduct electricity purchase cost.","The case conditions are that the microgrid marginal cost is higher than that of GENCO and that WEM has no congestion.","Retain the source formula units and case basis; the source does not specify the settlement duration."],["C_k^{DEM}(t)=π_k^T(t)·min{|P_k^N(t)·ΔT|,E_k^T(t)}。","A positive value is electricity purchase cost, and a negative value is electricity sale income; the income amount is |C_k^{DEM}(t)|.","E_k^T(t) is the estimated transaction electricity quantity determined by the submitted quotation, and the billing quantity takes the smaller of this and the absolute net-load energy.","k and t are the region and time-period indices, respectively; ΔT is in hours, the quotation is in $/kWh, the electricity quantity is in kWh, and the result is in $."]],"members":[{"id":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","market_meaning":"The ESC administers settlement by calculating individual customer energy bills and ensuring that prosumers’ income balances consumers’ expenditure. Scope: Individual customer settlement in the ESC-operated P2P sharing community. Local P2P pricing determines the electricity bills that prosumers pay or receive as repayments and that consumers pay. Scope: Electricity-bill payments and repayments of prosumers and consumers contracted with the ESC. The source states that the P2P buying-price ceiling implies lower consumer electricity bills under P2P sharing than under P2G trading. Scope: Consumer electricity bills under P2P sharing compared with P2G trading. Condition: The P2P buying price never exceeds the price of electricity bought from the grid. Under this uncompensated pricing case, the source states that prosumer income is undermined while consumers benefit; prosumers may receive less income than without P2P sharing. Scope: The source’s explanation of prosumer income and consumer benefits under uncompensated SDR pricing. Condition: The compensating price is zero and the supply-to-demand ratio exceeds one, so both P2P prices equal the grid selling price. At every studied penetration level, zero compensation is reported to leave some prosumers paying higher electricity bills or receiving lower incomes when participating in P2P sharing. Scope: The reported sensitivity evaluation across the studied penetration levels of PV battery systems. Condition: The compensating price is zero in the reported evaluation. With an appropriate compensating price, modified local SDR pricing is stated to ensure economic benefits for every individual prosumer and consumer, with economic gains described in terms of bill savings or repayments. Scope: Individual prosumer and consumer financial outcomes under modified SDR pricing compared with conventional P2G trading. Condition: The compensating price is appropriately set. Billing is conducted 24 hours after actual electricity consumption using recorded import/export energy data from smart meters, for example half-hourly records. Scope: Customer billing in the proposed two-stage P2P sharing arrangement. The positive value is the amount of electricity bill the prosumer is required to pay. Scope: Prosumer electricity-bill values in the reported financial assessment. Condition: The reported prosumer bill value is positive. The negative value denotes income received by the prosumer. Scope: Prosumer electricity-bill values in the reported financial assessment. Condition: The reported prosumer bill value is negative.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Consumer electricity bills under P2P sharing compared with P2G trading. | Customer billing in the proposed two-stage P2P sharing arrangement. | Electricity-bill payments and repayments of prosumers and consumers contracted with the ESC. | Individual customer settlement in the ESC-operated P2P sharing community. | Individual prosumer and consumer financial outcomes under modified SDR pricing compared with conventional P2G trading. | Prosumer electricity-bill values in the reported financial assessment. | The reported sensitivity evaluation across the studied penetration levels of PV battery systems. | The source’s explanation of prosumer income and consumer benefits under uncompensated SDR pricing.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e002","v141-b-g01-e004","v141-b-g01-e005","v141-b-g01-e006","v141-b-g01-e013","v141-b-g02-E23","v141-b-g03-E13","v141-b-g04-E10","v141-b-g04-E13","v141-b-g04-E15","v141-b-g04-E17"]},"constraint_refs":["19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C002","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C003","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C004","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C005","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C006","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C007","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C008","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C009","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C010","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C011","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C012","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C013","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C014"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"bd880e891130c0f37fb74ec2eeadfc37b72ff840ddc75d6776e532b766029c31","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007","node_id":"settlement.payment","function":"settlement.payment","unit_id":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-c-R006::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Selling the awarded 15 MW in the DNEM earns the LA $2100 in Case 1. Scope: The LA's DNEM sale in the worked Case 1 example. Condition: Microgrids have higher marginal generation costs than GENCOs, and the WEM has no congestion.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"The LA's DNEM sale in the worked Case 1 example.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21"]},"constraint_refs":["23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"4fc3abd037fab995441d6bf714cf020b1abb05971c1141049c2f093d84e493b7","paper":"23-hierarchical_market_structure","rule_ref":"23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-c-R006","node_id":"settlement.payment","function":"settlement.payment","unit_id":"23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","market_meaning":"The local-market cost/revenue equals the CSP’s proposed signed purchase/selling price multiplied by the smaller of its absolute net-load energy and its estimated trading volume. A positive amount represents purchase cost; a negative amount represents selling revenue of the corresponding absolute value. Scope: The CSP’s modeled cost/revenue in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"The CSP’s modeled cost/revenue in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E07","v141-b-g02-E08"]},"constraint_refs":["41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"9b588782ee1ffca4bf213605a8168e192c21233715df8f85c60a62ce672d0712","paper":"41-data_center_local_markets","rule_ref":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009","node_id":"settlement.payment","function":"settlement.payment","unit_id":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["19-two_stage_battery_sharing","23-hierarchical_market_structure","41-data_center_local_markets"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e002","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":"264","section":"2.1. P2P energy sharing structure","anchor_text":"surplus PV power from prosumers can also be traded with consumers who do not have PV systems. PP A t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A third party entity named “energy sharing coordinator (ESC)” coordinates between customers and provides P2P sharing services, i.e. assuring the power balance and payment balance [13]. The ESC is the controller of the DERs as well as the local market operator. The ESC is located at the PCC (e.g. the low voltage substation). A smart meter is installed at the PCC, measuring the real-time net power consumption of the community, and this measurement is the input of the ESC. The ESC is communicated with the controllers of these small-scale batteries at the prosumer premises, and provides control signals to them via one-way communication. Through managing the battery charging/discharging, the ESC ensures the maximum use of local energy resources and the minimum amount of electricity fed back into the grid. Therefore the overall energy cost of the community is reduced, and the reduced cost is distributed to individual customers through a P2P pricing mechanism. The ESC also calculates energy bills of individual customers based on the P2P pricing mechanism, using the recorded import/export energy (e.g. half-hourly) from smart meters at the premises.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"A third party entity named “energy sharing coordinator (ESC)” coordinates between customers and provides P2P sharing services, i.e. assuring the power balance and payment balance [13]. The ESC is the controller of the DERs as well as the local market operator. The ESC is located at the PCC (e.g. the low voltage substation). A smart meter is installed at the PCC, measuring the real-time net power consumption of the community, and this measurement is the input of the ESC. The ESC is communicated with the controllers of these small-scale batteries at the prosumer premises, and provides control signals to them via one-way communication. Through managing the battery charging/discharging, the ESC ensures the maximum use of local energy resources and the minimum amount of electricity fed back into the grid. Therefore the overall energy cost of the community is reduced, and the reduced cost is distributed to individual customers through a P2P pricing mechanism. The ESC also calculates energy bills of individual customers based on the P2P pricing mechanism, using the recorded import/export energy (e.g. half-hourly) from smart meters at the premises."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e004","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":"264","section":"2.1. P2P energy sharing structure","anchor_text":"Details of the control framework and the pricing mechanism for the local market are presented in Sec","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"(1) Energy sharing coordinator (ESC)\n• Export power to (or import power from) the main grid to ensure the power balance of the community;\n• Contract with a supplier for the electricity trade at pre-defined exporting/importing prices and ensure the payment balance between the community and the supplier;\n• Control the charging/discharging activities of all batteries;\n• Contract with individual prosumers and consumers according to the P2P pricing mechanism and ensure the prosumers’ income balance with the consumers’ expenditure.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"(1) Energy sharing coordinator (ESC)\n• Export power to (or import power from) the main grid to ensure the power balance of the community;\n• Contract with a supplier for the electricity trade at pre-defined exporting/importing prices and ensure the payment balance between the community and the supplier;\n• Control the charging/discharging activities of all batteries;\n• Contract with individual prosumers and consumers according to the P2P pricing mechanism and ensure the prosumers’ income balance with the consumers’ expenditure."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e005","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":"264","section":"2.1. P2P energy sharing structure","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 226 (2018) 261–276 the power balance of the community; Contract with a supplier for t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"(2) Prosumers\n• Hand over the control of their batteries to the ESC;\n• Contract with the ESC, pay/repay their electricity bills according to the P2P pricing mechanism (The prices are calculated 24 h after the actual consumption according to the predefined pricing mechanism).","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"(2) Prosumers\n• Hand over the control of their batteries to the ESC;\n• Contract with the ESC, pay/repay their electricity bills according to the P2P pricing mechanism (The prices are calculated 24 h after the actual consumption according to the predefined pricing mechanism)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e006","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":"264","section":"2.1. P2P energy sharing structure","anchor_text":"p g ( p he after the actual consumption according to the predeﬁned pricing is mechanism). (3) Consum","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"(3) Consumers\n• Contract with the ESC, pay their electricity bill according to the P2P pricing mechanism (The prices are calculated 24 h after the actual consumption according to the predefined pricing mechanism).","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"(3) Consumers\n• Contract with the ESC, pay their electricity bill according to the P2P pricing mechanism (The prices are calculated 24 h after the actual consumption according to the predefined pricing mechanism)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p11-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":11,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"sharing than under the P2G energy trading. Hence, there is no need to ti evaluate the participation ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"For the consumers, as the P2P buying price (Pr_{buy}^t) is never higher than the price of electricity bought from the grid (\\lambda_{buy,t}), the energy bill of consumers will be always lower when participating in P2P energy sharing than under the P2G energy trading. Hence, there is no need to evaluate the participation willingness of consumers.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"For the consumers, as the P2P buying price (Pr_{buy}^t) is never higher than the price of electricity bought from the grid (\\lambda_{buy,t}), the energy bill of consumers will be always lower when participating in P2P energy sharing than under the P2G energy trading. Hence, there is no need to evaluate the participation willingness of consumers."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"prices would dynamically change according to Eqs. (19) and (20). The relation of the P2P buying/sell","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"When SDR^{t} = 0, there is no selling power in the community, and all the required energy is bought from the main grid. The P2P selling and buying prices are both equal to the price of electricity bought from the grid (i.e. Pr_{sell}^{t} = Pr_{buy}^{t} = \\lambda_{buy,t}). When SDR^{t} = 1, the selling power is equal to the buying power in the community, and no power is required to import from (or export to) the main grid. The P2P selling and buying prices are both equal to the price of electricity sold to the grid with a compensation (i.e. Pr_{sell}^{t} = Pr_{buy}^{t} = \\lambda_{sell,t} + \\lambda). Here, \\lambda is a compensating price (0 \\le \\lambda \\le \\lambda_{buy,t} - \\lambda_{sell,t}), and it is used to compensate the prosumers to ensure the prosumers be better off when they participate in P2P energy sharing. When 0 \u003c SDR^{t} \u003c 1, or SDR^{t} > 1, the P2P selling and buying prices would dynamically change according to Eqs. (19) and (20). The reason for introducing the compensating price is that, when there is no compensating price (i.e. \\lambda = 0), both P2P selling and buying prices are equal to the price of electricity sold to grid when the SDR is more than 1. This will undermine the prosumers' income, but provide economic benefits to the consumers (i.e. these who do not have PV battery systems). It might happen that the prosumers receive lower incomes than the case of not participating in P2P energy sharing.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"When SDR^{t} = 0, there is no selling power in the community, and all the required energy is bought from the main grid. The P2P selling and buying prices are both equal to the price of electricity bought from the grid (i.e. Pr_{sell}^{t} = Pr_{buy}^{t} = \\lambda_{buy,t}). When SDR^{t} = 1, the selling power is equal to the buying power in the community, and no power is required to import from (or export to) the main grid. The P2P selling and buying prices are both equal to the price of electricity sold to the grid with a compensation (i.e. Pr_{sell}^{t} = Pr_{buy}^{t} = \\lambda_{sell,t} + \\lambda). Here, \\lambda is a compensating price (0 \\le \\lambda \\le \\lambda_{buy,t} - \\lambda_{sell,t}), and it is used to compensate the prosumers to ensure the prosumers be better off when they participate in P2P energy sharing. When 0 \u003c SDR^{t} \u003c 1, or SDR^{t} > 1, the P2P selling and buying prices would dynamically change according to Eqs. (19) and (20). The reason for introducing the compensating price is that, when there is no compensating price (i.e. \\lambda = 0), both P2P selling and buying prices are equal to the price of electricity sold to grid when the SDR is more than 1. This will undermine the prosumers' income, but provide economic benefits to the consumers (i.e. these who do not have PV battery systems). It might happen that the prosumers receive lower incomes than the case of not participating in P2P energy sharing."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p14-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":14,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"battery, but it only requires to reduce the price of the battery system to ev £430/kWh (only 14% low","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-14.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"When the compensating price is zero, at all the penetration levels of PV battery systems, some prosumers are paying more electricity bills or gaining lower incomes when participating in P2P energy sharing. This is because the economic beneﬁts were obtained by the consumers, whilst for some of the prosumers, it may not be worth participating in P2P energy sharing.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"When the compensating price is zero, at all the penetration levels of PV battery systems, some prosumers are paying more electricity bills or gaining lower incomes when participating in P2P energy sharing. This is because the economic beneﬁts were obtained by the consumers, whilst for some of the prosumers, it may not be worth participating in P2P energy sharing."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p15-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":15,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"pr This paper proposed a two-stage control method to realize P2P energy sharing in community Microgr","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-15.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"With an appropriate setting of the compensating price, the modiﬁed supply demand ratio based mechanism ensures every individual customer in the community be better oﬀcompared to the conventional P2G energy trading, i.e. every individual prosumer and consumer gain economic beneﬁts. This pricing mechanism can be used as a benchmark applicable to any P2P energy sharing model.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"With an appropriate setting of the compensating price, the modiﬁed supply demand ratio based mechanism ensures every individual customer in the community be better oﬀcompared to the conventional P2G energy trading, i.e. every individual prosumer and consumer gain economic beneﬁts. This pricing mechanism can be used as a benchmark applicable to any P2P energy sharing model."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p14-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":14,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"to evolutionary algorithms to achieve the global optimum for P2P energy sharing is considered as one","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-14.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"The unfairness of economic gains may exist, i.e. there are diﬀerences in bill savings (or repayments) among consumers (or prosumers).","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"The unfairness of economic gains may exist, i.e. there are diﬀerences in bill savings (or repayments) among consumers (or prosumers)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e013","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":3,"printed_page":"263","section":"1. Introduction","anchor_text":"in munity point of view, e.g. [19]. A thorough assessment method is ﬀ required to analyze the beneﬁt","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"A novel two-stage aggregated control framework was proposed to realize P2P energy sharing in low voltage distribution networks. Comparing to the previous works of P2P energy sharing with a third party entity, this control framework requires for very limited measurements and a one-way communication, providing a feasible and practical solution to P2P energy sharing. Compared to the P2P energy sharing without an intermedia, there is no need of fast communication between the market operator and the individual DERs, because the pricing and billing are conducted 24 h after the actual electricity consumption, using the recorded import/export energy (e.g. half-hourly) data from smart meters. Also, there are no divergence issues.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"A novel two-stage aggregated control framework was proposed to realize P2P energy sharing in low voltage distribution networks. Comparing to the previous works of P2P energy sharing with a third party entity, this control framework requires for very limited measurements and a one-way communication, providing a feasible and practical solution to P2P energy sharing. Compared to the P2P energy sharing without an intermedia, there is no need of fast communication between the market operator and the individual DERs, because the pricing and billing are conducted 24 h after the actual electricity consumption, using the recorded import/export energy (e.g. half-hourly) data from smart meters. Also, there are no divergence issues."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p14-c2r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":14,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Applied Energy 226 (2018) 261–276 f- means the amount of income the prosumer receives. It is found t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-14.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For the prosumers, a positive value means the amount of electricity bill the prosumer is required to pay, and a negative value means the amount of income the prosumer receives.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"For the prosumers, a positive value means the amount of electricity bill the prosumer is required to pay, and a negative value means the amount of income the prosumer receives."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p09-c2r4__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"buy , t ∗ Pim , t λ sell , t ∗ Pex , t ) dt (35) BP 2G = ∫t = 1 (λ 7) Power PPV ty PM EBC PL 8) EBD ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The cost of energy for individual premises with P2G trading is calculated by BP_{2G}^i = \\int_{t=1}^{T} (\\lambda_{buy,t} * P_{im,t}^i - \\lambda_{sell,t} * P_{ex,t}^i) dt \\tag{35}.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"The cost of energy for individual premises with P2G trading is calculated by BP_{2G}^i = \\int_{t=1}^{T} (\\lambda_{buy,t} * P_{im,t}^i - \\lambda_{sell,t} * P_{ex,t}^i) dt \\tag{35}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E08","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p10-c1r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"C. Long et al. where BPi 2G is the energy bill for customer i under P2G energy trading. i positive.F","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For consumers, P_{ex,t}^i is equal to zero, therefore BP_{2G}^i is always positive. For prosumers, BP_{2G}^i can be positive or negative. When BP_{2G}^i is positive, this means this is the amount of electricity bill the customer needs to pay. When BP_{2G}^i is negative, this means this is the amount of income the customer will be repaid.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"For consumers, P_{ex,t}^i is equal to zero, therefore BP_{2G}^i is always positive. For prosumers, BP_{2G}^i can be positive or negative. When BP_{2G}^i is positive, this means this is the amount of electricity bill the customer needs to pay. When BP_{2G}^i is negative, this means this is the amount of income the customer will be repaid."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p10-c1r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"C. Long et al. where BPi 2G is the energy bill for customer i under P2G energy trading. i positive.F","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The cost of energy for individual customers with P2P energy sharing is calculated by BP_{2P}^i = \\int_{t=1}^{T} [(NP_t^i - P_{aggBC,t}^i + P_{aggBD,t}^i) \\cdot Pr^t] dt \\tag{36}; where BP_{2P}^i is the energy bill for customer i under P2P energy sharing. Pr^t is the P2P trading price, and the value of the Pr^t depends on the role (i.e. consumer or producer) the customer is playing at time t.","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"The cost of energy for individual customers with P2P energy sharing is calculated by BP_{2P}^i = \\int_{t=1}^{T} [(NP_t^i - P_{aggBC,t}^i + P_{aggBD,t}^i) \\cdot Pr^t] dt \\tag{36}; where BP_{2P}^i is the energy bill for customer i under P2P energy sharing. Pr^t is the P2P trading price, and the value of the Pr^t depends on the role (i.e. consumer or producer) the customer is playing at time t."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p10-c2r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Applied Energy 226 (2018) 261–276 g. ⎧ Prsellt , NPti −PaggBCi , t + PaggBDi , t \u003c 0 e. t ⎪ Pr = 0 ,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Therefore, Pr^t is calculated by Pr^t = \\left\\{ \\begin{array}{ll} Pr_{sell}^t, & NP_t^i - P_{aggBC,t}^i + P_{aggBD,t}^i \u003c 0 \\\\ Pr_{buy}^t, & NP_t^i - P_{aggBC,t}^i + P_{aggBD,t}^i > 0 \\end{array} \\right. \\tag{37}","paper":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"Therefore, Pr^t is calculated by Pr^t = \\left\\{ \\begin{array}{ll} Pr_{sell}^t, & NP_t^i - P_{aggBC,t}^i + P_{aggBD,t}^i \u003c 0 \\\\ Pr_{buy}^t, & NP_t^i - P_{aggBC,t}^i + P_{aggBD,t}^i > 0 \\end{array} \\right. \\tag{37}"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-fbc18f73e3-p07-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":"1872","section":"IV. Case Study","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SMART GRID, VOL. 7, NO. 4, JULY 2016 TABLE III BIDDING STRATEGY AND AWARDED DIS","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table III","image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"TABLE III BIDDING STRATEGY AND AWARDED DISPATCH IN CASE 1 | Bidding Strategy: MG1 3.74, MG2 4, LA 1.05, G1 3.64, G2 4 | Awarded Dispatch (MW): MG1 40, MG2 20, LA 15 (imports power), G1 140, G2 135.","paper":"23-hierarchical_market_structure","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"TABLE III BIDDING STRATEGY AND AWARDED DISPATCH IN CASE 1 | Bidding Strategy: MG1 3.74, MG2 4, LA 1.05, G1 3.64, G2 4 | Awarded Dispatch (MW): MG1 40, MG2 20, LA 15 (imports power), G1 140, G2 135."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-fbc18f73e3-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":"1872","section":"IV. Case Study","anchor_text":"ed. market. re, Case 3) Lower marginal cost of electricity in microgrids of with no congestion in th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table III","image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Case 1) Higher Marginal Cost of Electricity in Microgrids With No Congestion in the Wholesale Electricity Market: In this case, the microgrids provide higher marginal cost for electricity compared to the GENCOs as shown in Table II. The bidding strategies and the awarded dispatches for each market participant are listed in Table III. Since there is no congestion in the WEM, the MCP is 80 $/MWh, while the MCP in the DNEM is 140 $/MWh. In this case, the lower price of electricity in the WEM provides incentives for the LA to import electricity from this market to the DNEM.","paper":"23-hierarchical_market_structure","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"Case 1) Higher Marginal Cost of Electricity in Microgrids With No Congestion in the Wholesale Electricity Market: In this case, the microgrids provide higher marginal cost for electricity compared to the GENCOs as shown in Table II. The bidding strategies and the awarded dispatches for each market participant are listed in Table III. Since there is no congestion in the WEM, the MCP is 80 $/MWh, while the MCP in the DNEM is 140 $/MWh. In this case, the lower price of electricity in the WEM provides incentives for the LA to import electricity from this market to the DNEM."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-fbc18f73e3-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":"1873","section":"IV. Case Study","anchor_text":"$1200 to purchase 15 MW electricity and earned $2100 by selling the same volume in the DNEM. The exc","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 4","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"$900, $8800, and $8604, respectively. Hence, the LA spent $1200 to purchase 15 MW electricity and earned $2100 by selling the same volume in the DNEM. The exchanged power, the LMP, and the bidding strategies of the market participants in the WEM and the DNEM are illustrated in Fig. 4. In the first iteration an arbitrary large number is assigned as the LMP at the LAs bus in the WEM (1000 $/MWh) and LA bids into the DNEM based on the assigned LMP at the WEM. The awarded dispatch imported to DNEM and the MCP at the DNEM is determined.","paper":"23-hierarchical_market_structure","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"$900, $8800, and $8604, respectively. Hence, the LA spent $1200 to purchase 15 MW electricity and earned $2100 by selling the same volume in the DNEM. The exchanged power, the LMP, and the bidding strategies of the market participants in the WEM and the DNEM are illustrated in Fig. 4. In the first iteration an arbitrary large number is assigned as the LMP at the LAs bus in the WEM (1000 $/MWh) and LA bids into the DNEM based on the assigned LMP at the WEM. The awarded dispatch imported to DNEM and the MCP at the DNEM is determined."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1.3 Energy trading cost/revenue from the local energy markets","anchor_text":"3.1.3. Energy trading cost/revenue from the local energy markets For each IDC, its net-load at the t","equation_id":"Eq. (14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$P_k^N(t)=P_k^{IDC}(t)+P_k^{CH/DIS}(t)-P_k^{RES}(t)$ (12)\n$P_k^{RES}(t)=\\beta_k^{RES}(t)\\cdot P_k^{RES}$ (13)\nA positive value of $P_k^N(t)$ indicates that the IDC needs to buy energy from external sources (i.e. the local energy market and/or the grid), while a negative value indicates that the IDC has surplus energy to sell to other customers. On the basis of the CSP’s proposed energy purchase/selling price in the kth local energy market, the corresponding amount of energy that the CSP can buy from/sell to the market can be estimated following Eq. (7). Then, the cost/revenue of the CSP from the kth local energy market is calculated as:\n$C_k^{DEM}(t)=\\pi_k^T(t)\\cdot\\min\\left\\{|P_k^N(t)\\cdot\\Delta T|, E_k^T(t)\\right\\}$ (14)","paper":"41-data_center_local_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"$P_k^N(t)=P_k^{IDC}(t)+P_k^{CH/DIS}(t)-P_k^{RES}(t)$ (12)\n$P_k^{RES}(t)=\\beta_k^{RES}(t)\\cdot P_k^{RES}$ (13)\nA positive value of $P_k^N(t)$ indicates that the IDC needs to buy energy from external sources (i.e. the local energy market and/or the grid), while a negative value indicates that the IDC has surplus energy to sell to other customers. On the basis of the CSP’s proposed energy purchase/selling price in the kth local energy market, the corresponding amount of energy that the CSP can buy from/sell to the market can be estimated following Eq. (7). Then, the cost/revenue of the CSP from the kth local energy market is calculated as:\n$C_k^{DEM}(t)=\\pi_k^T(t)\\cdot\\min\\left\\{|P_k^N(t)\\cdot\\Delta T|, E_k^T(t)\\right\\}$ (14)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E08","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1.3 / 3.1.4 Energy trading and grid purchase costs","anchor_text":"C. Guo et al. Consisting with the sign of πTk(t), when CDEMk (t) is positive, it repre­ sents the CS","equation_id":"Eq. (15)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Consisting with the sign of $\\pi_k^T(t)$, when $C_k^{DEM}(t)$ is positive, it represents the CSP’s energy purchase cost in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot; when it is negative, it means that the CSP sells energy to the market with the revenue of $|C_k^{DEM}(t)|$.","paper":"41-data_center_local_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","quote":"Consisting with the sign of $\\pi_k^T(t)$, when $C_k^{DEM}(t)$ is positive, it represents the CSP’s energy purchase cost in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot; when it is negative, it means that the CSP sells energy to the market with the revenue of $|C_k^{DEM}(t)|$."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["clearing_price_sales_receipt","individual_energy_bill_settlement","signed_price_capped_volume_accounting"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"KG40-L8-SC-L8-e260aa47afe769d8","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L8","ontological_kind":"SC-L8-e260aa47afe769d8"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"Calculate payable charges or receivable income according to the price and electricity-transaction billing quantity specified by the source, retaining separately the sign, measurement basis, quantity limit, and accumulation scope.","identity_fields":["Economic purpose: calculate purchase and sale electricity transaction payments","Main object: individual electricity bill, sales revenue, or local transaction income and expenditure","Responsibility relationship: determine the meaning of receipt and payment according to the participant's purchase or sale role"]},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","paper_id":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","source_occurrences":[{"id":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","market_meaning":"The ESC administers settlement by calculating individual customer energy bills and ensuring that prosumers’ income balances consumers’ expenditure. Scope: Individual customer settlement in the ESC-operated P2P sharing community. Local P2P pricing determines the electricity bills that prosumers pay or receive as repayments and that consumers pay. Scope: Electricity-bill payments and repayments of prosumers and consumers contracted with the ESC. The source states that the P2P buying-price ceiling implies lower consumer electricity bills under P2P sharing than under P2G trading. Scope: Consumer electricity bills under P2P sharing compared with P2G trading. Condition: The P2P buying price never exceeds the price of electricity bought from the grid. Under this uncompensated pricing case, the source states that prosumer income is undermined while consumers benefit; prosumers may receive less income than without P2P sharing. Scope: The source’s explanation of prosumer income and consumer benefits under uncompensated SDR pricing. Condition: The compensating price is zero and the supply-to-demand ratio exceeds one, so both P2P prices equal the grid selling price. At every studied penetration level, zero compensation is reported to leave some prosumers paying higher electricity bills or receiving lower incomes when participating in P2P sharing. Scope: The reported sensitivity evaluation across the studied penetration levels of PV battery systems. Condition: The compensating price is zero in the reported evaluation. With an appropriate compensating price, modified local SDR pricing is stated to ensure economic benefits for every individual prosumer and consumer, with economic gains described in terms of bill savings or repayments. Scope: Individual prosumer and consumer financial outcomes under modified SDR pricing compared with conventional P2G trading. Condition: The compensating price is appropriately set. Billing is conducted 24 hours after actual electricity consumption using recorded import/export energy data from smart meters, for example half-hourly records. Scope: Customer billing in the proposed two-stage P2P sharing arrangement. The positive value is the amount of electricity bill the prosumer is required to pay. Scope: Prosumer electricity-bill values in the reported financial assessment. Condition: The reported prosumer bill value is positive. The negative value denotes income received by the prosumer. Scope: Prosumer electricity-bill values in the reported financial assessment. Condition: The reported prosumer bill value is negative.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Consumer electricity bills under P2P sharing compared with P2G trading. | Customer billing in the proposed two-stage P2P sharing arrangement. | Electricity-bill payments and repayments of prosumers and consumers contracted with the ESC. | Individual customer settlement in the ESC-operated P2P sharing community. | Individual prosumer and consumer financial outcomes under modified SDR pricing compared with conventional P2G trading. | Prosumer electricity-bill values in the reported financial assessment. | The reported sensitivity evaluation across the studied penetration levels of PV battery systems. | The source’s explanation of prosumer income and consumer benefits under uncompensated SDR pricing.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e002","v141-b-g01-e004","v141-b-g01-e005","v141-b-g01-e006","v141-b-g01-e013","v141-b-g02-E23","v141-b-g03-E13","v141-b-g04-E10","v141-b-g04-E13","v141-b-g04-E15","v141-b-g04-E17"]},"constraint_refs":["19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C002","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C003","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C004","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C005","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C006","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C007","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C008","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C009","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C010","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C011","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C012","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C013","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C014"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"bd880e891130c0f37fb74ec2eeadfc37b72ff840ddc75d6776e532b766029c31"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The ESC administers settlement by calculating individual customer energy bills and ensuring that prosumers’ income balances consumers’ expenditure. Scope: Individual customer settlement in the ESC-operated P2P sharing community.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e002","v141-b-g01-e004"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e002","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":"264","section":"2.1. P2P energy sharing structure","anchor_text":"surplus PV power from prosumers can also be traded with consumers who do not have PV systems. PP A t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A third party entity named “energy sharing coordinator (ESC)” coordinates between customers and provides P2P sharing services, i.e. assuring the power balance and payment balance [13]. The ESC is the controller of the DERs as well as the local market operator. The ESC is located at the PCC (e.g. the low voltage substation). A smart meter is installed at the PCC, measuring the real-time net power consumption of the community, and this measurement is the input of the ESC. The ESC is communicated with the controllers of these small-scale batteries at the prosumer premises, and provides control signals to them via one-way communication. Through managing the battery charging/discharging, the ESC ensures the maximum use of local energy resources and the minimum amount of electricity fed back into the grid. Therefore the overall energy cost of the community is reduced, and the reduced cost is distributed to individual customers through a P2P pricing mechanism. The ESC also calculates energy bills of individual customers based on the P2P pricing mechanism, using the recorded import/export energy (e.g. half-hourly) from smart meters at the premises."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e004","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":"264","section":"2.1. P2P energy sharing structure","anchor_text":"Details of the control framework and the pricing mechanism for the local market are presented in Sec","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"(1) Energy sharing coordinator (ESC)\n• Export power to (or import power from) the main grid to ensure the power balance of the community;\n• Contract with a supplier for the electricity trade at pre-defined exporting/importing prices and ensure the payment balance between the community and the supplier;\n• Control the charging/discharging activities of all batteries;\n• Contract with individual prosumers and consumers according to the P2P pricing mechanism and ensure the prosumers’ income balance with the consumers’ expenditure."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local P2P pricing determines the electricity bills that prosumers pay or receive as repayments and that consumers pay. Scope: Electricity-bill payments and repayments of prosumers and consumers contracted with the ESC.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e005","v141-b-g01-e006"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e005","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":"264","section":"2.1. P2P energy sharing structure","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 226 (2018) 261–276 the power balance of the community; Contract with a supplier for t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"(2) Prosumers\n• Hand over the control of their batteries to the ESC;\n• Contract with the ESC, pay/repay their electricity bills according to the P2P pricing mechanism (The prices are calculated 24 h after the actual consumption according to the predefined pricing mechanism)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e006","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":"264","section":"2.1. P2P energy sharing structure","anchor_text":"p g ( p he after the actual consumption according to the predeﬁned pricing is mechanism). (3) Consum","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"(3) Consumers\n• Contract with the ESC, pay their electricity bill according to the P2P pricing mechanism (The prices are calculated 24 h after the actual consumption according to the predefined pricing mechanism)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C003","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"The source states that the P2P buying-price ceiling implies lower consumer electricity bills under P2P sharing than under P2G trading. Scope: Consumer electricity bills under P2P sharing compared with P2G trading. Condition: The P2P buying price never exceeds the price of electricity bought from the grid.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E13"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p11-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":11,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"sharing than under the P2G energy trading. Hence, there is no need to ti evaluate the participation ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"For the consumers, as the P2P buying price (Pr_{buy}^t) is never higher than the price of electricity bought from the grid (\\lambda_{buy,t}), the energy bill of consumers will be always lower when participating in P2P energy sharing than under the P2G energy trading. Hence, there is no need to evaluate the participation willingness of consumers."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C004","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Under this uncompensated pricing case, the source states that prosumer income is undermined while consumers benefit; prosumers may receive less income than without P2P sharing. Scope: The source’s explanation of prosumer income and consumer benefits under uncompensated SDR pricing. Condition: The compensating price is zero and the supply-to-demand ratio exceeds one, so both P2P prices equal the grid selling price.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E23"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"prices would dynamically change according to Eqs. (19) and (20). The relation of the P2P buying/sell","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"When SDR^{t} = 0, there is no selling power in the community, and all the required energy is bought from the main grid. The P2P selling and buying prices are both equal to the price of electricity bought from the grid (i.e. Pr_{sell}^{t} = Pr_{buy}^{t} = \\lambda_{buy,t}). When SDR^{t} = 1, the selling power is equal to the buying power in the community, and no power is required to import from (or export to) the main grid. The P2P selling and buying prices are both equal to the price of electricity sold to the grid with a compensation (i.e. Pr_{sell}^{t} = Pr_{buy}^{t} = \\lambda_{sell,t} + \\lambda). Here, \\lambda is a compensating price (0 \\le \\lambda \\le \\lambda_{buy,t} - \\lambda_{sell,t}), and it is used to compensate the prosumers to ensure the prosumers be better off when they participate in P2P energy sharing. When 0 \u003c SDR^{t} \u003c 1, or SDR^{t} > 1, the P2P selling and buying prices would dynamically change according to Eqs. (19) and (20). The reason for introducing the compensating price is that, when there is no compensating price (i.e. \\lambda = 0), both P2P selling and buying prices are equal to the price of electricity sold to grid when the SDR is more than 1. This will undermine the prosumers' income, but provide economic benefits to the consumers (i.e. these who do not have PV battery systems). It might happen that the prosumers receive lower incomes than the case of not participating in P2P energy sharing."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C005","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"At every studied penetration level, zero compensation is reported to leave some prosumers paying higher electricity bills or receiving lower incomes when participating in P2P sharing. Scope: The reported sensitivity evaluation across the studied penetration levels of PV battery systems. Condition: The compensating price is zero in the reported evaluation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-E10"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p14-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":14,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"battery, but it only requires to reduce the price of the battery system to ev £430/kWh (only 14% low","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-14.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"When the compensating price is zero, at all the penetration levels of PV battery systems, some prosumers are paying more electricity bills or gaining lower incomes when participating in P2P energy sharing. This is because the economic beneﬁts were obtained by the consumers, whilst for some of the prosumers, it may not be worth participating in P2P energy sharing."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C006","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"With an appropriate compensating price, modified local SDR pricing is stated to ensure economic benefits for every individual prosumer and consumer, with economic gains described in terms of bill savings or repayments. Scope: Individual prosumer and consumer financial outcomes under modified SDR pricing compared with conventional P2G trading. Condition: The compensating price is appropriately set.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-E15","v141-b-g04-E17"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p15-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":15,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"pr This paper proposed a two-stage control method to realize P2P energy sharing in community Microgr","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-15.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"With an appropriate setting of the compensating price, the modiﬁed supply demand ratio based mechanism ensures every individual customer in the community be better oﬀcompared to the conventional P2G energy trading, i.e. every individual prosumer and consumer gain economic beneﬁts. This pricing mechanism can be used as a benchmark applicable to any P2P energy sharing model."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p14-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":14,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"to evolutionary algorithms to achieve the global optimum for P2P energy sharing is considered as one","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-14.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"The unfairness of economic gains may exist, i.e. there are diﬀerences in bill savings (or repayments) among consumers (or prosumers)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C007","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Billing is conducted 24 hours after actual electricity consumption using recorded import/export energy data from smart meters, for example half-hourly records. Scope: Customer billing in the proposed two-stage P2P sharing arrangement.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e013"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e013","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":3,"printed_page":"263","section":"1. Introduction","anchor_text":"in munity point of view, e.g. [19]. A thorough assessment method is ﬀ required to analyze the beneﬁt","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"A novel two-stage aggregated control framework was proposed to realize P2P energy sharing in low voltage distribution networks. Comparing to the previous works of P2P energy sharing with a third party entity, this control framework requires for very limited measurements and a one-way communication, providing a feasible and practical solution to P2P energy sharing. Compared to the P2P energy sharing without an intermedia, there is no need of fast communication between the market operator and the individual DERs, because the pricing and billing are conducted 24 h after the actual electricity consumption, using the recorded import/export energy (e.g. half-hourly) data from smart meters. Also, there are no divergence issues."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C008","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The positive value is the amount of electricity bill the prosumer is required to pay. Scope: Prosumer electricity-bill values in the reported financial assessment. Condition: The reported prosumer bill value is positive.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-E13"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C008","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p14-c2r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":14,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Applied Energy 226 (2018) 261–276 f- means the amount of income the prosumer receives. It is found t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-14.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For the prosumers, a positive value means the amount of electricity bill the prosumer is required to pay, and a negative value means the amount of income the prosumer receives."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C009","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The negative value denotes income received by the prosumer. Scope: Prosumer electricity-bill values in the reported financial assessment. Condition: The reported prosumer bill value is negative.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-E13"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C009","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p14-c2r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":14,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Applied Energy 226 (2018) 261–276 f- means the amount of income the prosumer receives. It is found t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-14.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"For the prosumers, a positive value means the amount of electricity bill the prosumer is required to pay, and a negative value means the amount of income the prosumer receives."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C010","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The individual P2G energy bill for customer i is the time integral over T of the grid buying price times imported power minus the grid selling price times exported power. Scope: Individual P2G settlement baseline used in the source’s comparison with P2P sharing.","expression":"BP_{2G}^{i} = \\int_{t=1}^{T} \\left( \\lambda_{buy,t} P_{im,t}^{i} - \\lambda_{sell,t} P_{ex,t}^{i} \\right) dt","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta_lambda_buy","theta_lambda_sell","theta_P_im","theta_P_ex","theta_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E07","v141-b-g03-E08"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C010","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p09-c2r4__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"buy , t ∗ Pim , t λ sell , t ∗ Pex , t ) dt (35) BP 2G = ∫t = 1 (λ 7) Power PPV ty PM EBC PL 8) EBD ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The cost of energy for individual premises with P2G trading is calculated by BP_{2G}^i = \\int_{t=1}^{T} (\\lambda_{buy,t} * P_{im,t}^i - \\lambda_{sell,t} * P_{ex,t}^i) dt \\tag{35}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E08","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p10-c1r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"C. Long et al. where BPi 2G is the energy bill for customer i under P2G energy trading. i positive.F","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For consumers, P_{ex,t}^i is equal to zero, therefore BP_{2G}^i is always positive. For prosumers, BP_{2G}^i can be positive or negative. When BP_{2G}^i is positive, this means this is the amount of electricity bill the customer needs to pay. When BP_{2G}^i is negative, this means this is the amount of income the customer will be repaid."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C011","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The individual P2P energy bill for customer i is the time integral over T of the customer’s net position after aggregate battery charging/discharging multiplied by the role-dependent P2P trading price. Scope: Individual customer settlement under P2P sharing.","expression":"BP_{2P}^{i} = \\int_{t=1}^{T} \\left( NP_{t}^{i} - P_{aggBC,t}^{i} + P_{aggBD,t}^{i} \\right) Pr^{t} \\, dt","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta_NP","theta_P_aggBC","theta_P_aggBD","theta_Pr","theta_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E09"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C011","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p10-c1r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"C. Long et al. where BPi 2G is the energy bill for customer i under P2G energy trading. i positive.F","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The cost of energy for individual customers with P2P energy sharing is calculated by BP_{2P}^i = \\int_{t=1}^{T} [(NP_t^i - P_{aggBC,t}^i + P_{aggBD,t}^i) \\cdot Pr^t] dt \\tag{36}; where BP_{2P}^i is the energy bill for customer i under P2P energy sharing. Pr^t is the P2P trading price, and the value of the Pr^t depends on the role (i.e. consumer or producer) the customer is playing at time t."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C012","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The P2P trading price used in the individual bill is the P2P selling price when the customer’s net position after aggregate battery charging/discharging is negative and the P2P buying price when that net position is positive. Scope: Role-dependent P2P price selection in individual settlement. The source does not print a case for a zero net position.","expression":"Pr^{t} = \\begin{cases} Pr_{sell}^{t}, & NP_{t}^{i} - P_{aggBC,t}^{i} + P_{aggBD,t}^{i} \u003c 0 \\\\ Pr_{buy}^{t}, & NP_{t}^{i} - P_{aggBC,t}^{i} + P_{aggBD,t}^{i} > 0 \\end{cases}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta_Pr","theta_Pr_sell","theta_Pr_buy","theta_NP","theta_P_aggBC","theta_P_aggBD"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E09","v141-b-g03-E10"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C012","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p10-c1r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"C. Long et al. where BPi 2G is the energy bill for customer i under P2G energy trading. i positive.F","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The cost of energy for individual customers with P2P energy sharing is calculated by BP_{2P}^i = \\int_{t=1}^{T} [(NP_t^i - P_{aggBC,t}^i + P_{aggBD,t}^i) \\cdot Pr^t] dt \\tag{36}; where BP_{2P}^i is the energy bill for customer i under P2P energy sharing. Pr^t is the P2P trading price, and the value of the Pr^t depends on the role (i.e. consumer or producer) the customer is playing at time t."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p10-c2r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Applied Energy 226 (2018) 261–276 g. ⎧ Prsellt , NPti −PaggBCi , t + PaggBDi , t \u003c 0 e. t ⎪ Pr = 0 ,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Therefore, Pr^t is calculated by Pr^t = \\left\\{ \\begin{array}{ll} Pr_{sell}^t, & NP_t^i - P_{aggBC,t}^i + P_{aggBD,t}^i \u003c 0 \\\\ Pr_{buy}^t, & NP_t^i - P_{aggBC,t}^i + P_{aggBD,t}^i > 0 \\end{array} \\right. \\tag{37}"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C013","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"When the compensating price is zero and the supply-to-demand ratio exceeds one, both P2P selling and buying prices equal the grid selling price. Scope: Price consequence under uncompensated SDR pricing. This formalizes the price-equality part of the source’s uncompensated-pricing statement; the associated income and consumer-benefit conclusions remain qualitative.","expression":"(\\lambda = 0 \\land SDR^{t} > 1) \\Rightarrow \\left( Pr_{sell}^{t} = Pr_{buy}^{t} = \\lambda_{sell,t} \\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta_lambda_comp","theta_SDR","theta_Pr_sell","theta_Pr_buy","theta_lambda_sell"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E23"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C013","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"prices would dynamically change according to Eqs. (19) and (20). The relation of the P2P buying/sell","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"When SDR^{t} = 0, there is no selling power in the community, and all the required energy is bought from the main grid. The P2P selling and buying prices are both equal to the price of electricity bought from the grid (i.e. Pr_{sell}^{t} = Pr_{buy}^{t} = \\lambda_{buy,t}). When SDR^{t} = 1, the selling power is equal to the buying power in the community, and no power is required to import from (or export to) the main grid. The P2P selling and buying prices are both equal to the price of electricity sold to the grid with a compensation (i.e. Pr_{sell}^{t} = Pr_{buy}^{t} = \\lambda_{sell,t} + \\lambda). Here, \\lambda is a compensating price (0 \\le \\lambda \\le \\lambda_{buy,t} - \\lambda_{sell,t}), and it is used to compensate the prosumers to ensure the prosumers be better off when they participate in P2P energy sharing. When 0 \u003c SDR^{t} \u003c 1, or SDR^{t} > 1, the P2P selling and buying prices would dynamically change according to Eqs. (19) and (20). The reason for introducing the compensating price is that, when there is no compensating price (i.e. \\lambda = 0), both P2P selling and buying prices are equal to the price of electricity sold to grid when the SDR is more than 1. This will undermine the prosumers' income, but provide economic benefits to the consumers (i.e. these who do not have PV battery systems). It might happen that the prosumers receive lower incomes than the case of not participating in P2P energy sharing."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C014","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"For a consumer with no export, if the P2P buying price never exceeds the grid buying price at each time, the consumer’s P2P energy bill is lower than the P2G bill. Scope: Consumer bill comparison under the P2P buying-price ceiling.","expression":"\\left( \\forall t,\\; Pr_{buy}^{t} \\le \\lambda_{buy,t} \\right) \\land \\left( \\forall t,\\; P_{ex,t}^{i} = 0 \\right) \\Rightarrow BP_{2P}^{i} \u003c BP_{2G}^{i}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta_Pr_buy","theta_lambda_buy","theta_P_ex"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E13","v141-b-g03-E08"],"ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C014","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p11-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":11,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"sharing than under the P2G energy trading. Hence, there is no need to ti evaluate the participation ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"For the consumers, as the P2P buying price (Pr_{buy}^t) is never higher than the price of electricity bought from the grid (\\lambda_{buy,t}), the energy bill of consumers will be always lower when participating in P2P energy sharing than under the P2G energy trading. Hence, there is no need to evaluate the participation willingness of consumers."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E08","location":{"location_id":"src-b91cee592e-p10-c1r1__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":10,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"C. Long et al. where BPi 2G is the energy bill for customer i under P2G energy trading. i positive.F","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-10.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For consumers, P_{ex,t}^i is equal to zero, therefore BP_{2G}^i is always positive. For prosumers, BP_{2G}^i can be positive or negative. When BP_{2G}^i is positive, this means this is the amount of electricity bill the customer needs to pay. When BP_{2G}^i is negative, this means this is the amount of income the customer will be repaid."}]}],"variables":[],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"theta_lambda_buy","symbol":"\\lambda_{buy,t}","meaning":"Price of electricity bought from the grid at time t; used in the P2G bill and the P2P buying-price ceiling.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency/kWh","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E07","v141-b-g03-E08","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_lambda_buy"},{"parameter_id":"theta_lambda_sell","symbol":"\\lambda_{sell,t}","meaning":"Price of electricity sold to the grid at time t; used in the P2G bill, the compensating-price bound, and the uncompensated-price equality.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency/kWh","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E07","v141-b-g03-E08","v141-b-g02-E23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_lambda_sell"},{"parameter_id":"theta_lambda_comp","symbol":"\\lambda","meaning":"Compensating price in the modified SDR pricing, bounded in the source by 0 \u003c= lambda \u003c= lambda_buy,t - lambda_sell,t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency/kWh","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E23","v141-b-g04-E09","v141-b-g04-E10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_lambda_comp"},{"parameter_id":"theta_P_im","symbol":"P_{im,t}^{i}","meaning":"Power imported from the grid by customer i at time t, as recorded by the individual premises smart meter for the P2G bill.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E07","v141-b-g03-E08"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_P_im"},{"parameter_id":"theta_P_ex","symbol":"P_{ex,t}^{i}","meaning":"Power exported to the grid by customer i at time t, as recorded by the individual premises smart meter for the P2G bill.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E07","v141-b-g03-E08"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_P_ex"},{"parameter_id":"theta_NP","symbol":"NP_{t}^{i}","meaning":"Net-load term for customer i at time t used in the P2P bill; the source defines NP_t^i for prosumers and Eq. (36) applies the term at individual-customer level.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e008","v141-b-g03-E09"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_NP"},{"parameter_id":"theta_P_aggBC","symbol":"P_{aggBC,t}^{i}","meaning":"Aggregate battery charging power term for customer i at time t entering the P2P individual bill, taken as a prior-stage/recorded settlement input.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E09","v141-b-g02-E17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_P_aggBC"},{"parameter_id":"theta_P_aggBD","symbol":"P_{aggBD,t}^{i}","meaning":"Aggregate battery discharging power term for customer i at time t entering the P2P individual bill, taken as a prior-stage/recorded settlement input.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"kW","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E09","v141-b-g02-E18"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_P_aggBD"},{"parameter_id":"theta_Pr_sell","symbol":"Pr_{sell}^{t}","meaning":"P2P selling price at time t from local SDR pricing, selected in the individual bill when the customer has a negative net position.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency/kWh","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g03-E10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_Pr_sell"},{"parameter_id":"theta_Pr_buy","symbol":"Pr_{buy}^{t}","meaning":"P2P buying price at time t from local SDR pricing, selected in the individual bill when the customer has a positive net position.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency/kWh","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E22","v141-b-g03-E10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_Pr_buy"},{"parameter_id":"theta_Pr","symbol":"Pr^{t}","meaning":"Role-dependent P2P trading price at time t used in the individual P2P bill and selected by Eq. (37).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency/kWh","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-E09","v141-b-g03-E10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_Pr"},{"parameter_id":"theta_SDR","symbol":"SDR^{t}","meaning":"Supply-to-demand ratio at the community Microgrid at time t, used as the pricing condition for the uncompensated-price case.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"dimensionless","indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_SDR"},{"parameter_id":"theta_T","symbol":"T","meaning":"Number of time slots over the operation time period, used as the integral horizon in the individual bill formulas.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"time slots","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e008"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::theta::theta_T"}]},{"unit_id":"23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","paper_id":"23-hierarchical_market_structure","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","source_occurrences":[{"id":"23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-c-R006::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Selling the awarded 15 MW in the DNEM earns the LA $2100 in Case 1. Scope: The LA's DNEM sale in the worked Case 1 example. Condition: Microgrids have higher marginal generation costs than GENCOs, and the WEM has no congestion.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"The LA's DNEM sale in the worked Case 1 example.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21"]},"constraint_refs":["23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"4fc3abd037fab995441d6bf714cf020b1abb05971c1141049c2f093d84e493b7"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Selling the awarded 15 MW in the DNEM earns the LA $2100 in Case 1. Scope: The LA's DNEM sale in the worked Case 1 example. Condition: Microgrids have higher marginal generation costs than GENCOs, and the WEM has no congestion.","expression":"\\mathrm{payment}^{DNEM}_{LA,Case1} = \\lambda_0^{Case 1} \\cdot P_l^{imp,Case 1} = 140\\ \\$/MWh \\times 15\\ MW = 2100\\ \\$","formalization_origin":"equivalent_translation","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-x-001"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R006-theta-001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21"],"ref":"23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-fbc18f73e3-p07-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":"1872","section":"IV. Case Study","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SMART GRID, VOL. 7, NO. 4, JULY 2016 TABLE III BIDDING STRATEGY AND AWARDED DIS","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table III","image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"TABLE III BIDDING STRATEGY AND AWARDED DISPATCH IN CASE 1 | Bidding Strategy: MG1 3.74, MG2 4, LA 1.05, G1 3.64, G2 4 | Awarded Dispatch (MW): MG1 40, MG2 20, LA 15 (imports power), G1 140, G2 135."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-fbc18f73e3-p07-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":"1872","section":"IV. Case Study","anchor_text":"ed. market. re, Case 3) Lower marginal cost of electricity in microgrids of with no congestion in th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table III","image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Case 1) Higher Marginal Cost of Electricity in Microgrids With No Congestion in the Wholesale Electricity Market: In this case, the microgrids provide higher marginal cost for electricity compared to the GENCOs as shown in Table II. The bidding strategies and the awarded dispatches for each market participant are listed in Table III. Since there is no congestion in the WEM, the MCP is 80 $/MWh, while the MCP in the DNEM is 140 $/MWh. In this case, the lower price of electricity in the WEM provides incentives for the LA to import electricity from this market to the DNEM."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-fbc18f73e3-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":"1873","section":"IV. Case Study","anchor_text":"$1200 to purchase 15 MW electricity and earned $2100 by selling the same volume in the DNEM. The exc","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 4","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"$900, $8800, and $8604, respectively. Hence, the LA spent $1200 to purchase 15 MW electricity and earned $2100 by selling the same volume in the DNEM. The exchanged power, the LMP, and the bidding strategies of the market participants in the WEM and the DNEM are illustrated in Fig. 4. In the first iteration an arbitrary large number is assigned as the LMP at the LAs bus in the WEM (1000 $/MWh) and LA bids into the DNEM based on the assigned LMP at the WEM. The awarded dispatch imported to DNEM and the MCP at the DNEM is determined."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-x-001","symbol":"P_l^{imp}","meaning":"Power imported to the DNEM by LA l, i.e. the LA's awarded DNEM sale/injection into the distribution network; in the worked Case 1 this quantity is 15 MW.","kind":"operational","domain":"real scalar [MW], bounded by the source's DNEM import limits in (22); the Case 1 awarded value is 15 MW","unit":"MW","indices":["l"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E000","v141-b-g01-E015","v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-c-R006::x::v141-c-R006-x-001"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R006-theta-001","symbol":"\\lambda_0^{Case 1}","meaning":"Reported DNEM market clearing price (MCP) in Case 1; in the DNEM formulation \\lambda_0 is the Lagrange multiplier of the generation/load-balance constraint (20).","value":{"kind":"numeric","number_value":140.0,"text_value":null},"unit":"$/MWh","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E015","v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-c-R006::theta::v141-c-R006-theta-001"}]},{"unit_id":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","paper_id":"41-data_center_local_markets","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","source_occurrences":[{"id":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R009","market_meaning":"The local-market cost/revenue equals the CSP’s proposed signed purchase/selling price multiplied by the smaller of its absolute net-load energy and its estimated trading volume. A positive amount represents purchase cost; a negative amount represents selling revenue of the corresponding absolute value. Scope: The CSP’s modeled cost/revenue in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"The CSP’s modeled cost/revenue in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E07","v141-b-g02-E08"]},"constraint_refs":["41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"9b588782ee1ffca4bf213605a8168e192c21233715df8f85c60a62ce672d0712"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R009-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The local-market cost/revenue equals the CSP’s proposed signed purchase/selling price multiplied by the smaller of its absolute net-load energy and its estimated trading volume. A positive amount represents purchase cost; a negative amount represents selling revenue of the corresponding absolute value. Scope: The CSP’s modeled cost/revenue in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot.","expression":"C_k^{DEM}(t) = \\pi_k^T(t) \\cdot \\min\\left\\{\\left|P_k^N(t)\\cdot\\Delta T\\right|, E_k^T(t)\\right\\}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R009-x-piT","v141-c-R009-x-ET","v141-c-R009-x-PN","v141-c-R009-x-CDEM"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R009-theta-DeltaT"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E07","v141-b-g02-E08"],"ref":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1.3 Energy trading cost/revenue from the local energy markets","anchor_text":"3.1.3. Energy trading cost/revenue from the local energy markets For each IDC, its net-load at the t","equation_id":"Eq. (14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$P_k^N(t)=P_k^{IDC}(t)+P_k^{CH/DIS}(t)-P_k^{RES}(t)$ (12)\n$P_k^{RES}(t)=\\beta_k^{RES}(t)\\cdot P_k^{RES}$ (13)\nA positive value of $P_k^N(t)$ indicates that the IDC needs to buy energy from external sources (i.e. the local energy market and/or the grid), while a negative value indicates that the IDC has surplus energy to sell to other customers. On the basis of the CSP’s proposed energy purchase/selling price in the kth local energy market, the corresponding amount of energy that the CSP can buy from/sell to the market can be estimated following Eq. (7). Then, the cost/revenue of the CSP from the kth local energy market is calculated as:\n$C_k^{DEM}(t)=\\pi_k^T(t)\\cdot\\min\\left\\{|P_k^N(t)\\cdot\\Delta T|, E_k^T(t)\\right\\}$ (14)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E08","location":{"location_id":"src-307a684414-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.1.3 / 3.1.4 Energy trading and grid purchase costs","anchor_text":"C. Guo et al. Consisting with the sign of πTk(t), when CDEMk (t) is positive, it repre­ sents the CS","equation_id":"Eq. (15)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Consisting with the sign of $\\pi_k^T(t)$, when $C_k^{DEM}(t)$ is positive, it represents the CSP’s energy purchase cost in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot; when it is negative, it means that the CSP sells energy to the market with the revenue of $|C_k^{DEM}(t)|$."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-x-piT","symbol":"\\pi_k^T(t)","meaning":"Energy purchase/selling price proposed by the CSP in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot; positive denotes a purchase price and negative denotes a selling price under the source sign convention.","kind":"operational","domain":"signed real price in $/kWh; positive purchase and negative selling per source sign convention","unit":"$/kWh","indices":["k","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g02-E04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::x::v141-c-R009-x-piT"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-x-ET","symbol":"E_k^T(t)","meaning":"Estimated energy purchase/selling volume under the proposed price \\pi_k^T(t) in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot.","kind":"operational","domain":"non-negative real energy volume, E_k^T(t) \\ge 0 per source","unit":"kWh","indices":["k","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g02-E04"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::x::v141-c-R009-x-ET"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-x-PN","symbol":"P_k^N(t)","meaning":"Net-load of the kth IDC at the tth time slot; positive means the IDC needs to buy energy from external sources and negative means surplus energy to sell.","kind":"operational","domain":"signed real power in kW","unit":"kW","indices":["k","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g02-E07"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::x::v141-c-R009-x-PN"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R009-x-CDEM","symbol":"C_k^{DEM}(t)","meaning":"Cost/revenue of the CSP in the kth local energy market at the tth time slot; positive represents purchase cost and negative represents selling revenue of |C_k^{DEM}(t)|.","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued monetary cost/revenue in $, defined by Eq. (14)","unit":"$","indices":["k","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g02-E07","v141-b-g02-E08"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::x::v141-c-R009-x-CDEM"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R009-theta-DeltaT","symbol":"\\Delta T","meaning":"Duration of each time slot (hour) in the modeled operation horizon.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"hour","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E07"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R009","declaration_ref":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::theta::v141-c-R009-theta-DeltaT"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","stage":"L8","subtype":"individual_energy_bill_settlement","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"ESC calculates the individual P2P bill: BP_{2P}^i=∫_{t=1}^{T}(NP_t^i-P_{aggBC,t}^i+P_{aggBD,t}^i)Pr^t dt. When the net position in the parentheses is negative, Pr_{sell}^t is used; when positive, Pr_{buy}^t is used; a positive bill value indicates payable charges, and a negative value indicates income.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: an individual customer's electricity bill","Core operation: multiply the signed net position by the corresponding purchase or sale price and integrate over time","Output: customer payable charges or receivable income"],"variation_fields":["Retain the negative sign before the charging term, the positive sign before the discharging term, and the integration range from 1 to T in the original formula","The original text does not list the price-selection case for a zero net position","Settlement is based on smart-meter import and export records after 24 h of consumption, and half-hour records may be used","ESC is responsible for bill management and ensures that prosumer income and consumer expenditure are balanced","P2G is only a comparison bill: BP_{2G}^i=∫_{t=1}^{T}(λ_{buy,t}P_{im,t}^i-λ_{sell,t}P_{ex,t}^i)dt","The original text reports that without compensation some prosumers' benefits are harmed; appropriate compensation can benefit all customers, but savings or income may be uneven","The original text gives BP_{2P}^i\u003cBP_{2G}^i for consumers without exports under the purchase-price cap condition, retained as the source text's comparison conclusion"],"constraint_refs":["19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C005","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C006","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C007","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C008","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C009","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C010","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C011","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C012","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C014"],"paper_id":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","template_code":"JOC"},{"unit_id":"23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","stage":"L8","subtype":"clearing_price_sales_receipt","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"In Case 1, the income of LA from selling the awarded import quantity in DNEM is recorded, according to the source text, as payment^{DNEM}_{LA,Case1}=λ_0^{Case1}*P_l^{imp,Case1}=140 $/MWh×15 MW=2100 $.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: monetary income from the sale of awarded electricity","Core relationship: payment calculated according to the market clearing price and the awarded sales quantity","Output: sales revenue, not net profit after deducting electricity purchase cost"],"variation_fields":["Receiving entity and market: LA, DNEM","Case parameters: λ_0=140 $/MWh, P_l^{imp}=15 MW, sales revenue 2100 $","Case conditions: the microgrid marginal cost is higher than that of GENCO, and WEM has no congestion","Retain the source formula units and case basis; the source text does not specify the settlement duration"],"constraint_refs":["23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001"],"paper_id":"23-hierarchical_market_structure","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","template_code":"JOC"},{"unit_id":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","stage":"L8","subtype":"signed_price_capped_volume_accounting","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"CSP's local market cost/income is calculated as C_k^{DEM}(t)=\\pi_k^T(t)\\cdot\\min\\{|P_k^N(t)\\cdot\\Delta T|,E_k^T(t)\\}. A positive value indicates electricity purchase cost, a negative value indicates electricity sale income, whose income amount is |C_k^{DEM}(t)|; E_k^T(t) is the estimated transaction electricity quantity under the quotation.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: monetary cost or income of local electricity purchase and sale","Operation: multiply the signed quotation by the smaller of the absolute net-load energy and the estimated transaction electricity quantity","Output: positive electricity purchase cost or negative electricity sale income"],"variation_fields":["Accounting entity: CSP","Region and time-period indices: k,t","Time-period length: \\Delta T, in hours","The quotation unit is $/kWh, the electricity quantity unit is kWh, and the accounting result is in $","The estimated transaction quantity E_k^T(t) is determined by the submitted quotation"],"constraint_refs":["41-data_center_local_markets::v141-c-R009::v141-c-R009-C001"],"paper_id":"41-data_center_local_markets","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","template_code":"JOC"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","retained_variants":["BP_{2P}^i=∫_{t=1}^{T}(NP_t^i−P_{aggBC,t}^i+P_{aggBD,t}^i)Pr^t dt; retain the negative sign before the charging term, the positive sign before the discharging term, and the integration range from 1 to T.","If the net position in the parentheses is negative, use Pr_{sell}^t; if positive, use Pr_{buy}^t; the price choice for a zero net position is not listed.","A positive bill value is payable, and a negative value is income; settlement is based on smart-meter import and export records after 24 h of consumption, and half-hour records may be used.","ESC manages bills and ensures that prosumer income and consumer expenditure are balanced.","P2G is only a comparison bill: BP_{2G}^i=∫_{t=1}^{T}(λ_{buy,t}P_{im,t}^i−λ_{sell,t}P_{ex,t}^i)dt.","The source reports that without compensation some prosumers are harmed; appropriate compensation can benefit all customers, but savings or income may be uneven.","The result BP_{2P}^i\u003cBP_{2G}^i for consumers without exports under the purchase-price cap condition is retained as the source's comparison conclusion."],"evidence_refs":["19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment::projection"]},{"unit_id":"23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","retained_variants":["Case 1：payment^{DNEM}_{LA,Case1}=λ_0^{Case1}*P_l^{imp,Case1}=140 $/MWh×15 MW=2100 $。","The income corresponds to LA selling the awarded import quantity in DNEM; it is sales revenue and does not deduct electricity purchase cost.","The case conditions are that the microgrid marginal cost is higher than that of GENCO and that WEM has no congestion.","Retain the source formula units and case basis; the source does not specify the settlement duration."],"evidence_refs":["23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment::projection"]},{"unit_id":"41-data_center_local_markets::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","retained_variants":["C_k^{DEM}(t)=π_k^T(t)·min{|P_k^N(t)·ΔT|,E_k^T(t)}。","A positive value is electricity purchase cost, and a negative value is electricity sale income; the income amount is |C_k^{DEM}(t)|.","E_k^T(t) is the estimated transaction electricity quantity determined by the submitted quotation, and the billing quantity takes the smaller of this and the absolute net-load energy.","k and t are the region and time-period indices, respectively; ΔT is in hours, the quotation is in $/kWh, the electricity quantity is in kWh, and the result is in $."],"evidence_refs":["41-data_center_local_markets::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment::projection"]}],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false,"baseline_parent_id":"COMP-a607cc256fbfc1eb","baseline_parent_code":"SET038","secondary_class":"SC-L8-e260aa47afe769d8","category":{"id":"SC-L8-e260aa47afe769d8","stage":"L8","label_zh":"Transaction consideration and bill settlement","definition_zh":"Determine, account for, or settle the monetary consideration and accounts of resource transactions, including amounts, settlement rates, and overall receipts and payments; charges, deviation adjustments, and pass-through arrangements within composite accounts are retained by source.","main_identity_axes":["Economic purpose: discharge the monetary-consideration obligation of a resource transaction","Primary objects: transaction sums, bills, net receipts and payments, or the basis for their settlement","Responsibility relation: receipt and payment by the transaction participants, which may include platform collection and pass-through"],"inclusion_criteria":["It principally specifies the payment, receipt, pricing basis, or bill settlement corresponding to a resource transaction.","Product differences such as electrical energy, storage use, and demand reduction may serve as source branches.","Composite units centered on transaction accounts are included as a whole, retaining the charge, deviation, and overall-net accounting bases."],"exclusion_criteria":["It only specifies the monetary meaning or accounting scope of an independent charge component.","Its primary function is collective cost allocation or distribution of cooperative gains.","It only specifies attribution of the operator's difference revenue or bearing of a funding shortfall.","It only describes that member payments are affected, and there is as yet no definite settlement relation or basis."],"supporting_unit_ids":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M02::settlement.payment","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::settlement.payment","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-a-M02-residual-balancing::settlement.payment","05-double_auction::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment"],"status":"agent_defined_structurally_validated"},"definition":{"definition_zh":"Calculate payable charges or receivable income according to the price and electricity-transaction billing quantity specified by the source, retaining separately the sign, measurement basis, quantity limit, and accumulation scope.","identity_fields":["Economic purpose: calculate purchase and sale electricity transaction payments","Main object: individual electricity bill, sales revenue, or local transaction income and expenditure","Responsibility relationship: determine the meaning of receipt and payment according to the participant's purchase or sale role"],"member_unit_ids":["19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","23-hierarchical_market_structure::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","41-data_center_local_markets::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment"]}},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/199"});
